---
source: tests/cli.rs
expression: "String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout)"
---
Import a GPG key and configure git commit and tag signing, non-interactively, for CI and workstations

Usage: gpg-import [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Examples:
  # Import the key in GPG_PRIVATE_KEY and preset the passphrase in GPG_PASSPHRASE
  $ gpg-import

  # Pipe a key straight from gpg
  $ gpg --armor --export-secret-key batman@dc.com | gpg-import --key -

  # Read the key and passphrase from files (only one of them can read stdin)
  $ gpg-import --key @/path/to/private.key --passphrase @/path/to/passphrase

  # Configure git globally instead of the current repository
  $ gpg-import --git-global-config

  # Sign with a specific subkey. List fingerprints with
  # `gpg --list-secret-keys --with-subkey-fingerprints` and pick one marked [S]
  $ gpg-import --fingerprint "$SUBKEY_FINGERPRINT"

  # Turn on tag signing, leaving commit and push signing as they are
  $ gpg-import --sign tags

  # Use a different committer identity from the one in the key
  $ gpg-import --git-committer-name "Bruce Wayne" --git-committer-email "bruce@wayne.enterprises"

  # Preview the import without changing anything
  $ gpg-import --dry-run

Commands:
  version  Print build time version information

Options:
  -k, --key <GPG_KEY>
          An ASCII armored GPG private key (optionally base64 encoded). Use - for stdin or @path to read from a file
          
          Don't pass the key itself as the value: an armored key starts with -----BEGIN, which is read as an option, and the key would be visible to other processes. Use GPG_PRIVATE_KEY, - or @path instead
          
          [env: GPG_PRIVATE_KEY]

  -p, --passphrase <PASSPHRASE>
          The passphrase of the GPG private key if set. Use - for stdin or @path to read from a file
          
          A passphrase given directly is visible to other processes, e.g. through ps. Use GPG_PASSPHRASE, - or @path instead
          
          [env: GPG_PASSPHRASE]

  -f, --fingerprint <FINGERPRINT>
          The fingerprint of a specific key or subkey to use for signing
          
          [env: GPG_FINGERPRINT=]

  -t, --trust-level <TRUST_LEVEL>
          A level of trust to associate with the GPG private key

          Possible values:
          - 1: I don't know or won't say
          - 2: I do NOT trust
          - 3: I trust marginally
          - 4: I trust fully
          - 5: I trust ultimately
          
          [env: GPG_TRUST_LEVEL=]

  -s, --skip-git
          Skip all GPG configuration for the detected git repository
          
          [env: GPG_SKIP_GIT=]

      --git-global-config
          Apply git signing configuration globally
          
          [env: GPG_GIT_GLOBAL_CONFIG=]

      --git-committer-name <NAME>
          Override the committer name instead of using the value from the GPG key
          
          [env: GPG_GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=]

      --git-committer-email <EMAIL>
          Override the committer email. If it matches another uid on the GPG key, that uid's name is used too
          
          [env: GPG_GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=]

      --dry-run
          Simulate the import without making changes
          
          [env: GPG_DRY_RUN=]

      --skip-verify
          Skip the test signature that proves the imported key can sign
          
          [env: GPG_SKIP_VERIFY=]

      --sign <OPERATIONS>
          The git operations to sign, comma separated. Settings for the others are left as they are

          Possible values:
          - commits: commit.gpgsign
          - tags:    tag.gpgsign
          - pushes:  push.gpgsign (if-asked)
          
          [env: GPG_SIGN=]
          [default: commits,tags,pushes]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
